    {'path': 'documentation/WORDS_L410_sun_fades_20261019.md', 'mode': 'edit', 'base_md5': 'a2adf3a282e6ee186ce515f7cdd8269d', 'new_md5': '689db385ca3c2b138796d323330c6f67', 'zone': None, 'edits': [('\n**1d, the sentence 3c adds at the end of the panel text.**\nThe corona is very hot, up to about {CORONA_TEMPERATURE_MAX_K} kelvin,\nfar hotter than the surface below it (NASA).\n\n', '\n**1c, marked 2026-10-19 (build session).** No source we have opened\nsays the helmets sit over the magnetic equator: Suess and Nerney\n(2004) say only that they are the closed-field regions, and neither\nGemini worksheet of 2026-10-19 has a row on the streamer belt. Tony:\n"drop for now but keep a breadcrumb." Approved line:\nThe streamer belt, drawn with it, is the part of the corona where the\nSun\'s magnetic field closes in loops.\nBreadcrumb: "over the Sun\'s magnetic equator" waits for a source; it\nis recorded on the ledger (L-410) so a later relay can ask for it.\n\n**1d, the sentence 3c adds at the end of the panel text.**\nThe corona is very hot, up to about {CORONA_TEMPERATURE_MAX_K} kelvin,\nfar hotter than the surface below it (NASA).\n\n**1d, marked 2026-10-19 (build session).** Confirmed by Tony as\ndrafted. The page was re-opened before marking: "Sun: Facts - NASA\nScience", https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/ -- "The corona reaches\nup to 3.5 million deg F (2 million deg C) - much, much hotter than the\nphotosphere." 2 million degrees Celsius is 2 million kelvin to the one\nfigure printed.\n\n'), ('Alfven surface.\n\n', 'Alfven surface.\n\n**1e, marked 2026-10-19 (build session).** Tony: "it\'s a bit\nconfusing. the same issue arises here, distinguishing between the\ncorona \'dots\' and the streamer belt. also, the alfven surface is not\ndefined." The helmet heights move to the streamer belt\'s own words,\nwhere their citation prints (a website note holds only drawing\nchoices); "over the Sun\'s magnetic equator" goes, as in 1c. Approved,\n"yes, clearer. approved.":\nThe corona is drawn in two parts. The dots show how dense its gas is\nat each height, on average, drawn the same in every direction. The\nstreamer belt, drawn as a band, shows the brightest part of the\ncorona, where the Sun\'s magnetic field closes in loops. The dots stop\nat the Alfven surface, the outer edge of the Sun\'s atmosphere, where\nthe gas becomes the solar wind.\n\n')]},
    {'path': 'documentation/WORDS_L410_build_list_20261019.md', 'mode': 'new', 'new_md5': '55bec9a00b64402fb7014c993876ad6a', 'content': '<!-- Doc-Kind: hand | The build\'s visitor words for the Sun\'s two fades (L-410 with L-131): what the approved list did not already settle, for Tony to mark before the patches are saved. -->\n# Words: the Sun\'s two fades, the build\'s list\n\nBuilt on orrery 1b4733509558535e1d0db54471a05523a1f1952a at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/palomas_orrery and gallery\n4fc320fa6d2e0e10d9a50b8b69006cbff96a1364 at\nhttps://github.com/tonylquintanilla/tonyquintanilla.github.io. Nothing\nhere is in either repository yet.\nTony\'s local folders: orrery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\palomas_orrery_for_github\\\ngallery\nC:\\Users\\tonyq\\OneDrive\\Desktop\\python_work\\tonyquintanilla.github.io\\\n\nType: BUILD, words for review. Written October 19-20, 2026 by\nAnthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5. The approved words are in\n`WORDS_L410_sun_fades_20261019.md` (items 1a to 3c, with tonight\'s\nmarks on 1c, 1d and 1e). This list holds only what the build added or\nhad to change. Answer each item in place: "-- yes", "-- change to\n...", "-- let\'s discuss".\n\n## 1. Four small changes to the approved words\n\n**1.1 The temperature prints as 2,000,000.** The approved sentence is\n"up to about {CORONA_TEMPERATURE_MAX_K} kelvin". The store prints a\nnumber by its figures, so it reads "up to about 2,000,000 kelvin", not\n"2 million" as I said earlier tonight. The website\'s Sun room already\nprints its other temperatures the same way. Keep 2,000,000, or should\nI add a way to print "2 million"? -- yes. keep the numbers. sometimes the numbers are a bit overwhelming, but not 2 million. we would have to talk about it. \n\n**1.2 A missing word, fixed.** 3b read "Asteroids give less than\n{ZODIACAL_DUST_ASTEROID_MAX_PERCENT} (Nesvorny and others, 2010)." It\nnow reads "less than 10 percent". A simple error, fixed and reported. -- yes\n\n**1.3 The dust cloud\'s hover adds kilometres.** The orrery\'s hovers\ngive every distance in km beside AU, so 2c\'s "the drawing stops at 5.2\nAU" reads there "the drawing stops at 5.2 AU (780,000,000 km)". The\nwebsite prints 2c as approved. -- yes\n\n**1.4 The Sun\'s summary hover, two lines.** The hover on the Sun\nitself lists the corona\'s layers. Two of its lines named the old\nspheres.\n\nOld:\n- Five corona/boundary layers now visualized separately:\n- \\* Inner Corona (K-corona): 1-3 R_sun, ~1-3 million K\n- \\* Extended Corona (F-corona): 50 R_sun, a boundary chosen for the\n  drawing\n\nNew:\n- The corona, the boundaries in it, and the dust beyond, each drawn on\n  its own:\n- \\* Corona: dots from the surface to the Alfven surface, thinning with\n  height -- yes\n- \\* Zodiacal Dust Cloud (F-corona): dust out to 5.2 AU, flattened\n  toward the planets\' plane -- yes, and add the km parenthetically\n\nThe rest of that hover is unchanged. Its paragraph on comet MAPS still\nnames "Extended F-corona" and "Inner K-corona", with typed numbers; it\nbelongs to L-411\'s typed numbers and is recorded there.\n\n## 2. The names in the orrery\n\n- Checkboxes: "-- Corona" (replacing "-- Inner Corona", "-- Streamer\n  Belt" and "-- Extended Corona (F-corona)") and "-- Zodiacal Dust\n  Cloud (F-corona)". Both from 1a and 2a. -- yes\n- Legend entries: "Sun: Corona" (one entry for the dots and the\n  streamer belt) and "Sun: Zodiacal Dust Cloud (F-corona)". -- yes\n- The streamer belt\'s info marker keeps its own hover, unchanged\n  tonight. Its words come to you as their own list when its shape is\n  next touched (L-411), as revision 3 said. -- yes\n\n## 3. Two drawing choices the sources do not settle\n\n**3.1 How the dust thins near the Sun.** Lamy and others (2022) give\nthe two edges: the dust starts to thin at about 19 solar radii and is\ngone by about 5. They do not give the shape in between. I drew it as a\nstraight line: the density is multiplied by a factor that rises evenly\nfrom nothing at 5 solar radii to full at 19. The approved words already\nsay "thins from about 19 solar radii inward and is gone by about 5", so\nno words change. Recommended: the straight line, as the simplest shape\nthat matches both edges. -- yes. the illustration does not zoom in to see it.  \n\n**3.2 Where the dust cloud is centred.** The COBE model puts the\ncloud\'s centre slightly off the Sun, about 0.013 AU (Kelsall\'s Table\n1). That is about 3 solar radii: invisible at the cloud\'s own scale,\nbut a visible shift of the dust-free hole when you zoom in to the\ncorona. I drew the cloud centred on the Sun and did not say so.\nRecommended: keep it centred, because near the Sun the cloud\'s shape\nis warped anyway (Lamy and others), which the words already say the\ndrawing does not show. -- yes\n\n## 4. The citations a visitor reads -- yes\n\nOn the website the i panel prints each number\'s citation word for word\n(What a Visitor Reads). These are the new rows, each read tonight from\nan open copy. The citation is exactly as it will be served.\n\n| Row | Value | Citation |\n|---|---|---|\n| The corona model\'s temperature | 1,180,000 K | Mann, Warmuth, Vocks and Rouillard (2023), "A heliospheric density and magnetic field model", A&A 679, A64, https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2023/11/aa45050-22/aa45050-22.html -- Sect. 3: the solar wind\'s density from Parker\'s equation (eqs. 9 and 10), "from the bottom of the corona up to a heliocentric distance of 250 solar radii", "in the sense of a long-term average global model"; "point M is given by Ce = 9.127 x 10^34 s^-1 and T = 1.18 MK." |\n| The model\'s mean particle mass | 0.57 proton masses | Mann, Warmuth, Vocks and Rouillard (2023), A&A 679, A64 -- Sect. 2: "the mean value of 0.058 can be considered as typical for the ratio nu, leading to mu = 0.57". |\n| The model\'s electron flow | 9.127 x 10^34 per second | the same as the model\'s temperature |\n| The corona\'s temperature | 2,000,000 K | NASA Science, "Sun: Facts", https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/ -- the corona reaches up to 2 million degrees Celsius, "much, much hotter than the photosphere". |\n| How fast the dust thins | 1.34 | Kelsall, Weiland, Franz, Reach, Arendt, Dwek, Freudenreich, Hauser, Moseley, Odegard, Silverberg and Wright (1998), "The COBE Diffuse Infrared Background Experiment Search for the Cosmic Infrared Background: II. Model of the Interplanetary Dust Cloud", ApJ 508, 44, https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/9806250 -- Sect. 4.2, eqs. 6 and 7: the smooth cloud\'s density, n0 R^-alpha f(zeta), with f(zeta) = exp(-beta g^gamma); Table 1, "Density and Geometrical Parameters of the IPD Model": alpha, the radial power-law exponent, 1.34. |\n| The fan\'s three shape numbers | 4.14, 0.942, 0.189 | Kelsall et al. (1998), as above -- Sect. 4.2, eqs. 6 and 7, the smooth cloud; Table 1: beta, the vertical shape parameter, 4.14 (gamma, the vertical power-law exponent, 0.942; mu, the widening parameter, 0.189), each on its own row. |\n| The middle plane\'s tilt | 2.03 degrees | Kelsall et al. (1998), as above -- Table 1: i, the inclination of the midplane, 2.03 deg; the model is computed in heliocentric ecliptic coordinates (Sect. 4.2). |\n| The middle plane\'s node | 77.7 degrees | Kelsall et al. (1998), as above -- Table 1: Omega, the ascending node of the midplane, 77.7 deg. |\n| Where the dots stop | 5.2 AU | Kelsall et al. (1998), as above -- Sect. 4.2: "an outer radial cutoff of 5.2 AU from the Sun, which roughly corresponds to the orbit of Jupiter." |\n| Where the dust starts to thin | 19 solar radii | Lamy, Gilardy and Llebaria (2022), "Observations of the Solar F-corona from Space", Space Sci. Rev., doi 10.1007/s11214-022-00918-y, https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.11533 -- abstract: "The outer border of the depletion zone where interplanetary dust particles start to be affected by sublimation appears well constrained at 19Rsun. This zone extends down to 5Rsun, thus defining the boundary of the dust-free zone". |\n| The dust-free zone | 5 solar radii | the same, then: Parker Solar Probe\'s first signs of it: Howard, Vourlidas, Bothmer et al. (2019), "Near-Sun observations of an F-corona decrease and K-corona fine structure", Nature 576, 232-236, https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-019-1807-x -- abstract: the F-corona\'s intensity falls at short elongations, "suggestive of the long-sought dust-free zone". |\n| Where the dust comes from | 85, 95 and 10 percent | Nesvorny, Jenniskens, Levison, Bottke and Vokrouhlicky (2010), "Cometary Origin of the Zodiacal Cloud and Carbonaceous Micrometeorites", ApJ 713, 816, https://arxiv.org/abs/0909.4322 -- abstract: "We find that 85-95% of the observed mid-infrared emission is produced by particles from the Jupiter-family comets (JFCs) and <10% by dust from long period comets. Asteroidal dust is found to be present at <10%." |\n| Boltzmann\'s constant | 1.380649 x 10^-23 J/K | NIST, "CODATA Recommended Values of the Fundamental Physical Constants: 2022", https://www.physics.nist.gov/cuu/pdf/wall_2022.pdf -- "Boltzmann constant k 1.380 649 x 10^-23 (exact) J K^-1". |\n| The proton\'s mass | 1.67262192595 x 10^-27 kg | NIST, as above -- "proton mass m_p 1.672 621 925 95(52) x 10^-27 kg". |\n\nTwo notes. The corona model\'s three numbers, Boltzmann\'s constant and\nthe proton\'s mass are what the dots are placed from; the website\nprints none of them, but the i panel may show their citations. And\nMann and others\' Table 8, which you saved tonight, is the check: the\ndensity worked out from these rows sits 0.9 to 1.6 percent above the\ntable at every one of its 2,491 distances. That comparison is recorded\non the temperature row, not in its citation.\n\n## 5. What is built, and what is not yet\n\nBuilt in the orrery, not yet saved as a patch: the rows above; the two\nstored drawings and their checks (14 of 14 planted faults caught); the\ncorona\'s dots and the dust cloud drawn in the orrery, one checkbox and\none legend entry each; the old spheres and their words removed. The\norrery opens and runs headless; the provenance gate reads 0 Tier-1 and\nthe whole tree 280, as before.\n\nNot yet built: the website\'s half (the Sun room drawing the same dots,\nin your approved words), which follows the orrery\'s push, as Earth\'s\nfade did. L-191\'s 19 tooltip strings: three of them went tonight with\nthe old spheres (the inner corona\'s, the outer corona\'s and the\nstreamer belt\'s); the other sixteen are not touched.\n\nSession written October 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5.\n'},
    {'path': 'export_drawings.py', 'mode': 'edit', 'base_md5': '4c4de3b00dec9697086cdc8722b32c44', 'new_md5': 'a72df11407873e98d18322612a8ac849', 'zone': None, 'edits': [('    frame         the frame the coordinates are in\n    unit          "au" (the Hills cloud) or "r_earth" (the belts, the\n                  exosphere)\n    rounding      how each coordinate was rounded, and why that is safe\n', '    frame         the frame the coordinates are in\n    unit          "au" (the Hills cloud, the dust cloud), "r_earth" (the\n                  belts, the exosphere) or "r_sun" (the corona)\n    rounding      how each coordinate was rounded, and why that is safe\n'), ("    w, belt       each dot's weight, 0 to 1 (the belts: from the declared\n                  fade; the exosphere: from the density at its distance),\n                  and (the belts only) which belt it is in, 0 inner,\n", "    w, belt       each dot's weight, 0 to 1 (the belts: from the declared\n                  fade; the exosphere: from the density at its distance;\n                  the corona and the dust cloud: from the sunlight at its\n                  distance),\n                  and (the belts only) which belt it is in, 0 inner,\n"), ('earth_exosphere_sampler.py, in Earth radii, with each dot\'s weight.)\n"""\n', 'earth_exosphere_sampler.py, in Earth radii, with each dot\'s weight.)\nModule updated: October 19, 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5 (L-410\nwith L-131: the fourth and fifth stored drawings, the Sun\'s corona from\nsun_corona_sampler.py, in solar radii, and the zodiacal dust cloud from\nzodiacal_dust_sampler.py, in AU in the ecliptic frame, each dot with its\nweight, the sunlight reaching it.)\n"""\n'), ("\n# name -> function returning the file's content. A new stored drawing\n", '\ndef _sun_corona():\n    """The Sun\'s corona\'s file content, as a dict (L-410, 2026-10-19)."""\n    import sun_corona_sampler as scs\n    rows = scs.rows_from_store()\n    dots = scs.drawn_corona(rows)\n    return {\n        "schema": SCHEMA,\n        "drawing": "sun_corona",\n        "what": ("The Sun\'s corona as dots from the Sun\'s surface to the "\n                 "Alfven surface. A dot\'s place follows the electron "\n                 "density of Mann et al. (2023)\'s solar wind model; its "\n                 "weight, the sunlight reaching it, which falls with the "\n                 "square of distance. Drawn round, the model\'s long-term "\n                 "average; only the shape of the thinning is drawn."),\n        "made_by": "sun_corona_sampler.drawn_corona()",\n        "frame": ("centred on the Sun. The drawing is round, so no frame "\n                  "turn changes what a visitor sees."),\n        "unit": "r_sun",\n        "rounding": ("each coordinate to 0.001 solar radii, about 700 km, "\n                     "and each weight to 0.01: a drawing, not a "\n                     "measurement"),\n        "settings": dots["settings"],\n        "rows": list(scs.ROW_NAMES),\n        "rows_sha256": rows_sha256(rows),\n        "dots": len(dots["x"]),\n        "x": [round(v, 3) for v in dots["x"]],\n        "y": [round(v, 3) for v in dots["y"]],\n        "z": [round(v, 3) for v in dots["z"]],\n        "w": [round(v, 2) for v in dots["w"]],\n    }\n\n\ndef _zodiacal_dust():\n    """The zodiacal dust cloud\'s file content, as a dict (L-131,\n    2026-10-19)."""\n    import zodiacal_dust_sampler as zds\n    rows = zds.rows_from_store()\n    dots = zds.drawn_dust(rows)\n    return {\n        "schema": SCHEMA,\n        "drawing": "zodiacal_dust",\n        "what": ("The zodiacal dust cloud as dots from the edge of the "\n                 "dust-free zone to 5.2 AU. A dot\'s place follows the "\n                 "smooth cloud of Kelsall et al. (1998)\'s COBE model, "\n                 "flattened toward a middle plane tilted to the ecliptic, "\n                 "thinning near the Sun between the edges Lamy et al. "\n                 "(2022) give; its weight, the sunlight reaching it, which "\n                 "falls with the square of distance."),\n        "made_by": "zodiacal_dust_sampler.drawn_dust()",\n        "frame": ("ecliptic and equinox of J2000, centred on the Sun, the "\n                  "orrery\'s drawing frame"),\n        "unit": "au",\n        "rounding": ("each coordinate to 0.0001 AU, about 15,000 km, and "\n                     "each weight to 0.01: a drawing, not a measurement"),\n        "settings": dots["settings"],\n        "rows": list(zds.ROW_NAMES),\n        "rows_sha256": rows_sha256(rows),\n        "dots": len(dots["x"]),\n        "x": [round(v, 4) for v in dots["x"]],\n        "y": [round(v, 4) for v in dots["y"]],\n        "z": [round(v, 4) for v in dots["z"]],\n        "w": [round(v, 2) for v in dots["w"]],\n    }\n\n\n# name -> function returning the file\'s content. A new stored drawing\n'), ('    "earth_exosphere": _earth_exosphere,\n}\n', '    "earth_exosphere": _earth_exosphere,\n    "sun_corona": _sun_corona,\n    "zodiacal_dust": _zodiacal_dust,\n}\n')]},
    {'path': 'orrery_maintenance_run.py', 'mode': 'edit', 'base_md5': '92980fa558a4b141ef800731c8248e0d', 'new_md5': '2da835c04093ae5ab6f724bf7f8b8fad', 'zone': None, 'edits': [('and data/drawings/earth_exosphere.json, Earth\'s hydrogen exosphere.)\n"""\n', 'and data/drawings/earth_exosphere.json, Earth\'s hydrogen exosphere.)\nModule updated: October 20, 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5 (L-410\nwith L-131: and data/drawings/sun_corona.json and zodiacal_dust.json, the\nSun\'s corona and the zodiacal dust cloud.)\n"""\n'), ("      'data/drawings/van_allen_belts.json',\n      'data/drawings/earth_exosphere.json']),\n    ('Module atlas',    ['module_atlas.py'],\n", "      'data/drawings/van_allen_belts.json',\n      'data/drawings/earth_exosphere.json',\n      'data/drawings/sun_corona.json',\n      'data/drawings/zodiacal_dust.json']),\n    ('Module atlas',    ['module_atlas.py'],\n")]},
    {'path': 'palomas_orrery.py', 'mode': 'edit', 'base_md5': '009ebe038d99c280ce8b308ebbaf283f', 'new_md5': '53908b8476deb74771ca57142aa65f83', 'zone': None, 'edits': [("animation's Sun arrow is clamped to that view. Tony, 2026-10-19.)\n\n", 'animation\'s Sun arrow is clamped to that view. Tony, 2026-10-19.)\nModule updated: October 20, 2026 with Anthropic\'s Claude Opus 5.5 (L-410\nwith L-131: one "-- Corona" checkbox, sun_corona_var, for the corona\'s\nfade and its streamer belt, replacing Inner Corona, Streamer Belt and\nExtended Corona; and "-- Zodiacal Dust Cloud (F-corona)",\nsun_zodiacal_dust_var. The animation\'s static-only legend placeholders\nare one per checkbox. Tony\'s approved words, 2026-10-19 and 2026-10-20.)\n\n'), ('    termination_shock_info,\n    outer_corona_info,\n    inner_corona_info,\n    streamer_belt_info,\n    roche_limit_info,\n', '    termination_shock_info,\n    corona_info,\n    zodiacal_dust_info,\n    roche_limit_info,\n'), ("    if center_object_name != 'Sun':\n        for key, var in sun_shell_vars.items():\n            if var.get() == 1 and not _engine_animates('Sun', key):\n", "    if center_object_name != 'Sun':\n        # L-410 (2026-10-19): the corona's two drawings share one checkbox\n        # ('corona' and 'streamer_belt'), so one placeholder per checkbox.\n        _seen_vars = set()\n        for key, var in sun_shell_vars.items():\n            if id(var) in _seen_vars:\n                continue\n            _seen_vars.add(id(var))\n            if var.get() == 1 and not _engine_animates('Sun', key):\n"), ('sun_chromosphere_var = tk.IntVar(value=0)\nsun_inner_corona_var = tk.IntVar(value=0)\nsun_outer_corona_var = tk.IntVar(value=0)\nsun_streamer_belt_var  = tk.IntVar(value=0)\nsun_roche_limit_var    = tk.IntVar(value=0)\n', "sun_chromosphere_var = tk.IntVar(value=0)\n# L-410 (2026-10-19): one checkbox for the corona's fade and its streamer\n# belt, which replaces Inner Corona, Extended Corona and Streamer Belt;\n# and one for the zodiacal dust cloud (L-131).\nsun_corona_var = tk.IntVar(value=0)\nsun_zodiacal_dust_var = tk.IntVar(value=0)\nsun_roche_limit_var    = tk.IntVar(value=0)\n"), ("\n    'outer_corona': sun_outer_corona_var,\n    'inner_corona': sun_inner_corona_var,\n    'streamer_belt':  sun_streamer_belt_var,\n    'roche_limit':    sun_roche_limit_var,\n", "\n    'zodiacal_dust': sun_zodiacal_dust_var,\n    # L-410: the corona's two drawings share one checkbox.\n    'corona': sun_corona_var,\n    'streamer_belt':  sun_corona_var,\n    'roche_limit':    sun_roche_limit_var,\n"), ('    sun_chromosphere_var.set(state)\n    sun_inner_corona_var.set(state)\n    sun_outer_corona_var.set(state)\n    sun_streamer_belt_var.set(state)\n    sun_roche_limit_var.set(state)\n', '    sun_chromosphere_var.set(state)\n    sun_corona_var.set(state)\n    sun_zodiacal_dust_var.set(state)\n    sun_roche_limit_var.set(state)\n'), ('\nsun_inner_corona_checkbutton = tk.Checkbutton(shell_options_frame, text="-- Inner Corona", variable=sun_inner_corona_var)\nsun_inner_corona_checkbutton.pack(anchor=\'w\')\nCreateToolTip(sun_inner_corona_checkbutton, inner_corona_info)\n\n', '\nsun_corona_checkbutton = tk.Checkbutton(shell_options_frame, text="-- Corona", variable=sun_corona_var)\nsun_corona_checkbutton.pack(anchor=\'w\')\nCreateToolTip(sun_corona_checkbutton, corona_info)\n\n'), ('\nsun_streamer_belt_checkbutton = tk.Checkbutton(shell_options_frame,\n    text="-- Streamer Belt", variable=sun_streamer_belt_var)\nsun_streamer_belt_checkbutton.pack(anchor=\'w\')\nCreateToolTip(sun_streamer_belt_checkbutton, streamer_belt_info)\n\nsun_alfven_surface_checkbutton = tk.Checkbutton(shell_options_frame,\n    text="-- Alfven Surface", variable=sun_alfven_surface_var)\nsun_alfven_surface_checkbutton.pack(anchor=\'w\')\nCreateToolTip(sun_alfven_surface_checkbutton, alfven_surface_info)\n\nsun_outer_corona_checkbutton = tk.Checkbutton(shell_options_frame, text="-- Extended Corona (F-corona)", variable=sun_outer_corona_var)\nsun_outer_corona_checkbutton.pack(anchor=\'w\')\nCreateToolTip(sun_outer_corona_checkbutton, outer_corona_info)\n\n', '\nsun_alfven_surface_checkbutton = tk.Checkbutton(shell_options_frame,\n    text="-- Alfven Surface", variable=sun_alfven_surface_var)\nsun_alfven_surface_checkbutton.pack(anchor=\'w\')\nCreateToolTip(sun_alfven_surface_checkbutton, alfven_surface_info)\n\nsun_zodiacal_dust_checkbutton = tk.Checkbutton(shell_options_frame, text="-- Zodiacal Dust Cloud (F-corona)", variable=sun_zodiacal_dust_var)\nsun_zodiacal_dust_checkbutton.pack(anchor=\'w\')\nCreateToolTip(sun_zodiacal_dust_checkbutton, zodiacal_dust_info)\n\n')]},
    {'path': 'palomas_orrery_dashboard.py', 'mode': 'edit', 'base_md5': '068db90d4cb0297b0659a9343e80447a', 'new_md5': '0bda50fc7560ba7fd4a2d6da7bb66efa', 'zone': None, 'edits': [('         "draws as given -- the Hills cloud (hills_cloud_sampler.py), Earth\'s "\n         "radiation belts (van_allen_belt_sampler.py) and Earth\'s hydrogen "\n         "exosphere (earth_exosphere_sampler.py), each from its rows with a "\n         "fixed seed, the same dots the orrery window draws (L-421, Tony\'s "\n', '         "draws as given -- the Hills cloud (hills_cloud_sampler.py), Earth\'s "\n         "radiation belts (van_allen_belt_sampler.py), Earth\'s hydrogen "\n         "exosphere (earth_exosphere_sampler.py), the Sun\'s corona "\n         "(sun_corona_sampler.py) and the zodiacal dust cloud "\n         "(zodiacal_dust_sampler.py), each from its rows with a "\n         "fixed seed, the same dots the orrery window draws (L-421, Tony\'s "\n')]},
